@@ -134,6 +134,25 @@ from Rails Campfire, and compatibility limits, are listed below.
 <details>
 <summary>Differences from Rails Campfire</summary>
 
+- **Bots:** bot ownership and codes use an additive `chat_bots` table linked to ordinary users.
+  The Rust bot UI/API is not available when switching back to the older Rails image; existing
+  users, memberships, and messages remain compatible. Bot API bodies are literal UTF-8 text,
+  not HTML or form fields. A bot must belong to the destination room. Optional webhook URLs use
+  an additive `chat_bot_webhooks` table. Mention callbacks are best-effort background jobs (no
+  retries or persistence across crashes), allow internal HTTP(S) services, and never follow
+  redirects. Bots join open rooms only when "Add to all open rooms" is enabled; people still
+  join automatically. Open-room forms allow selecting individual bots. Turning the bot switch
+  off preserves memberships. A first non-direct room membership
+  sends a `joined` callback, whose text/HTML reply is handled like a mention reply. Additive
+  `chat_bot_auto_join` and `chat_bot_room_joins` tables store opt-ins and deduplicate greetings.
+  Bot-authored messages do not trigger callbacks. See [bot webhooks](docs/bot-webhooks.md).
+  Administrators can set **Bots per member** in account settings (default 5, zero allowed).
+  Non-deleted bots (including suspended bots) count; administrators are exempt. Lowering the limit preserves existing
+  bots. This Rust-only limit is stored as `bots_per_member` in the account settings JSON.
+  Fixed safety limits allow ten posts per ten seconds across API and webhook replies. Exceeding
+  that starts a thirty-second cooldown; five more attempts suspend the bot. Ten consecutive
+  failed webhook deliveries also suspend it. Suspended bots keep rooms/messages and require
+  administrator reactivation on the bots page. Safety state is persisted in additive tables.
 - **WebSockets:** `permessage-deflate` without context takeover compresses each broadcast once
   for all subscribers. Decoded messages remain identical.
 - **CSRF:** `Sec-Fetch-Site` replaces tokens. Writes accept `same-origin` and `same-site`, reject
