diff --git a/docs/bot-webhooks.md b/docs/bot-webhooks.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..afa7193
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bot-webhooks.md
@@ -0,0 +1,102 @@
+# Bot callbacks
+
+Bots are ordinary users, so existing room-scoped mention autocomplete and signed mention
+attachments work for bots without a separate frontend implementation. Only active bot members
+of the room receive callbacks; repeated mentions produce one callback per bot per message.
+Literal `@name` text is not a signed mention. Command text removes only the recipient's verified
+mention attachments, preserving other mentions and literal names. The HTML body is the full
+stored rich-text body.
+
+Webhook settings are owner/admin scoped, alongside the bot's name and avatar. The additive
+`chat_bot_webhooks` table allows upgrades of existing Rust and Rails databases without altering
+Rails tables. Empty input deletes the setting; omitted input preserves it. URLs must be absolute
+HTTP(S), without userinfo or fragments. Internal services are intentionally supported, so bot
+managers should be trusted to configure outbound HTTP destinations on the Campfire network.
+
+After a message is committed and broadcast, an in-memory job resolves recipients and sends JSON
+callbacks. Posting never awaits the HTTP call. Delivery uses the existing bounded best-effort
+job runner: no retries, and queued work can be lost on crashes or queue overflow. Within a job,
+up to four recipients run concurrently; failures are isolated per recipient. Each HTTP exchange
+has a 15-second total deadline and replies are limited to 1 MiB. Redirects are never followed,
+since the payload's `room.reply_to` contains the bot's secret posting credential. Do not log
+payloads or share that path with other services.
+
+Successful 2xx responses with `text/plain` or `text/html` posts a nonblank UTF-8 reply. Plain text is escaped;
+HTML uses the normal Action Text pipeline, including sanitization when rendered. HTTP 204, non-2xx
+status codes, and unsupported media types produce no message. Replies use normal message
+creation (including search indexing and push events) and room broadcasts. Bot identity, webhook
+configuration, and room membership are checked again before inserting a reply. Bot-authored
+messages never trigger callbacks, avoiding recursive bot-to-bot loops.
+
+The payload contains relative reply and deep-link paths. A bot can return 204 and later post
+literal UTF-8 text to `room.reply_to` using the existing bearer-code API.
+
+Regression command: `cargo test -p campfire bot_webhook`. Tests run a local HTTP service and gate
+its response until after the user's POST returns, exercise the payload and both reply types,
+follow the deep link, post out-of-band, and cover unsupported replies and revocation.
+
+## Memberships and joined callbacks
+
+Bots start with no open-room memberships. The new/edit form's **Add to all open rooms**
+checkbox is off by default. Enabling it grants all existing open rooms and opts into future
+open rooms (including rooms changed from closed to open). Disabling it removes only the opt-in,
+not existing memberships. Open-room new/edit forms provide bot checkboxes for selective access,
+while opted-in bots remain selected and disabled. Only bot memberships are changed by this
+picker; omitted picker parameters preserve memberships for older clients. Closed rooms keep
+the existing user picker. People still automatically join open rooms. Existing installations
+keep their existing bot memberships, with automatic joining off until explicitly enabled.
+
+`Room::grant_to` records a bot's first non-direct membership in `chat_bot_room_joins` and emits
+`Event::BotJoined` after commit. Both manual and automatic grants use this path. Unique
+bot/room keys prevent duplicate greetings on repeated saves, duplicate grants, or removal and
+re-addition. Direct conversations do not record or emit joins. No historical notifications
+are sent on upgrade, and configuring a webhook does not replay past joins.
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/42-Xy3abc9defGh/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+The joined job shares the mention HTTP delivery and reply pipeline, including timeouts,
+sanitization, permission rechecks, and bot-authored message broadcasts. There is no request
+host in this database-triggered job, so broadcasts render relative URLs. Deduplication is
+persistent, but network delivery retains the existing best-effort job semantics: no retries,
+and no guarantee of delivery after crashes, queue overflow, or endpoint failures. A bot
+without a webhook at delivery time receives no callback.
+
+Regression commands:
+
+- `cargo test -p campfire_db`
+- `cargo test -p campfire bots`
+
+## Automatic suspension
+
+Safety limits are fixed in code, per bot (not per room or posting source), and survive restarts.
+`chat_bot_safety` stores cooldown/failure/suspension state; `chat_bot_posts` stores at most ten
+recent accepted posting reservations per bot. Both are additive tables, created for existing
+installs on boot. They do not change the Rails user status or delete messages/memberships.
+
+- Ten accepted messages in a rolling ten-second window are allowed. API posts and webhook
+  replies reserve slots in the same database write transaction as message creation, so
+  concurrent requests cannot exceed the limit. Slots exactly ten seconds old expire.
+- The next message is rejected and starts thirty seconds of cooldown. API requests return
+  `429` with `Retry-After` rounded up to whole seconds. Replies are discarded. Five additional
+  attempts during that cooldown suspend the bot; the fifth gets `403`. The rejected message
+  which started cooldown is not one of those five. An expired cooldown does not retain strikes.
+- Non-2xx responses (including redirects), connection errors, and timeouts count as failed
+  deliveries. Any successful 2xx resets the consecutive failure streak, even when it has no
+  usable reply (blank, unsupported type, oversized, or invalid UTF-8). Reply-posting rejection
+  is not a failed delivery. Ten consecutive failures suspend the bot.
+- Suspended bots return `403` for posting and are skipped by both mention and joined callbacks.
+  Already-sent HTTP requests cannot be recalled; their replies cannot post after suspension.
+  Suspension is durable, retains owner quotas, and is visible with its reason on the bots page.
+- Only administrators can use **Reactivate**, with the existing Turbo/native `confirm()` flow.
+  Reactivation resets both counters, cooldown, and posting history. It increments a generation
+  number so pre-reactivation in-flight webhook results/replies cannot affect the new state.
+
+Regression coverage: `cargo test -p campfire bots` and
+`cargo test -p campfire_db models::bot`. The latter uses a frozen clock for exact window,
+rounded retry, and cooldown boundaries; the former covers real local HTTP failures, timeout,
+connection refusal, mixed API/reply flooding, and administrator-only reactivation.
